<p>Enterprises adopting MCP usually start with the core: clients, servers, tools and resources. Within months, requests arrive for more: interactive interfaces inside the chat, single sign-on instead of per-server logins, machine-to-machine access for batch agents, conventions for regulated data. MCP's answer to all of these is extensions. Understanding how they work is now part of designing an agent platform.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>Core, projects and extensions</h2>
<p>The MCP maintainers describe the ecosystem in three layers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The core specification</strong>: how clients and servers talk, and the minimum bar for interoperability.</li>
<li><strong>Projects</strong>: supporting infrastructure such as the Registry for discovering servers and the Inspector for testing.</li>
<li><strong>Extensions</strong>: optional patterns for specialised needs, built on top of the core.</li>
</ul>
<p>Extensions are strictly additive. They are negotiated during the initialisation handshake, and a client or server that doesn't recognise one simply skips it, so the baseline protocol keeps working<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. They are also how future protocol changes get validated: an extension that gains traction signals what might belong in the core<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What exists today</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th><th>Why an enterprise cares</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>UI</td><td>MCP Apps, the first official extension, for interactive interfaces in the conversation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup></td><td>Richer workflows; new security review surface. See <a href="/insights/mcp-apps-tool-annotations/">MCP Apps and tool annotations</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Authorisation</td><td>OAuth Client Credentials for machine-to-machine access, and Enterprise-Managed Authorization for central identity provider control<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup></td><td>Central policy, audit and onboarding through your identity provider</td></tr>
<tr><td>Domain conventions</td><td>Community groups exploring, for example, compliance metadata for financial services<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup></td><td>Shared semantics for regulated workflows, once mature</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The maintainers also draw a useful line: proprietary client features that happen to use MCP are not necessarily extensions, and they encourage implementers to adopt extensions as the standard way to add custom behaviour<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Prefer official extensions over client-specific features. The first works across clients you'll adopt next year; the second ties your workflows to one vendor.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I decide which to adopt</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Is it official, recommended or community-defined?</strong> Official extensions live in the MCP organisation and go through the SEP process<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Community extensions can be valuable but carry more change risk.</li>
<li><strong>Which of our clients support it?</strong> Check the extension support matrix<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup> against the clients your organisation actually uses. An extension supported by one client only is a pilot, not a standard.</li>
<li><strong>What's the security impact?</strong> UI extensions run code from servers inside hosts; auth extensions change who can access what. Both need review before approval.</li>
<li><strong>Does it degrade gracefully?</strong> Because extensions are optional, test what happens when a client doesn't support one. The workflow should still be safe, even if less convenient.</li>
<li><strong>Who owns it internally?</strong> Each supported extension needs an owner on the platform team, a version policy and a place in your server review checklist.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Auth extensions first</h2>
<p>If you adopt one extension category first, make it authorisation. Per-user, per-server OAuth doesn't scale in an enterprise, and machine-to-machine agents need a supported way to authenticate without a person in the loop. Centralising access through your identity provider gives you consistent policy and one audit trail, which supports the controls in <a href="/insights/securing-mcp-servers-checklist/">securing MCP servers</a> and the evidence expected in <a href="/insights/soc2-for-ai-startups/">SOC 2</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Contributing back</h2>
<p>Organisations in regulated sectors have a real opportunity here. If your industry needs shared conventions, such as provenance, consent or compliance metadata on tool calls, the SEP process offers an Extensions Track for proposing them<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Defining those conventions openly is better than every vendor inventing its own.</p>
</section>

